<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Sphinx of Giza</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Abu al-Hawl, Hor-em-akhet, Harmachis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sphinx">Sphinx</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a> is a limestone statue of a resting sphinx, with a human head and a lion's body, carved from the bedrock of the Giza Plateau on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Archaeological evidence suggests it was made in the Old Kingdom during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khafre">Khafre</a>, but whose face it shows remains in dispute. It is 73 m long, 20 m high and 19 m wide, the oldest known monumental sculpture in Egypt. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> it was revered as the sun god Hor-em-akhet. Its nose was deliberately damaged, though not by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s troops.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-sphinx-of-giza.jpg" alt="Great Sphinx of Giza" width="330" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hesham Ebaid · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sphinx_with_the_third_pyramid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sphinx">Sphinx</a> is a monolith carved from the limestone bedrock of the Eocene-aged Mokattam Formation, which also served as the quarry for the pyramids and other monuments at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>. It faces east, and is part of the Memphite Necropolis and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The archaeological evidence suggests it was created between 2600 and 2500 BCE for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a>, builder of the Great Pyramid, or his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khafre">Khafre</a>. Scholars believe the face represents Khufu or one of his sons, Djedefre and Khafre, but there is no consensus.</p><p>Rainer Stadelmann reads the style of the headdress and beard as more indicative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a>, and argues that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khafre">Khafre</a>'s Causeway was built to fit an existing structure that could only have been the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sphinx">Sphinx</a>. Farouk El-Baz has suggested that the head may have been carved first from a natural wind-sculpted ridge, with the surrounding ditch quarried later. The Old Kingdom name is unknown, as neither the enclosure nor the temple in front was finished, and the scarcity of Old Kingdom material suggests no cult was established then.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sphinx">Sphinx</a> measures 73 m from paw to tail, 20 m from the base to the top of the head, and 19 m across the rear haunches. Traces of red pigment remain on the face and yellow and blue elsewhere, which led Mark Lehner to suggest the monument was once decked out in gaudy comic book colours.</p><p>The nose was removed deliberately, and marks show rods or chisels hammered into the face. The popular legend that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s troops shot it off in 1798 is wrong, since it is documented as damaged since at least the 10th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a> the Giza Necropolis was abandoned and sand buried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sphinx">Sphinx</a> to its shoulders. About 1400 BCE the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iv">Thutmose IV</a> dug out the front paws and placed between them the Dream Stele, which calls the Sphinx Hor-em-akhet, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> of the Horizon. In the first century CE it was cleared again in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> and the governor Tiberius Claudius Balbilus, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> describes its face as coloured red. In the Middle Ages Arab writers described it as a talisman guarding the area from the desert.</p><p>In 1817 Giovanni Battista Caviglia led the first modern dig, which uncovered the chest completely, and Émile Baraize cleared the whole figure in 1926. In 1857 Auguste Mariette found the later Inventory Stela, whose story that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a> found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sphinx">Sphinx</a> already buried is contradicted by archaeology and considered a revision by local priests.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Sphinx of Giza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Step Pyramid of Saqqara, Sneferu's three pyramids at Meidum and Dahshur, the Great Pyramid of Khufu at Giza, with which the fully developed pyramid style was reached, Khafre's second pyramid and, in traditional thinking, the <b>Great Sphinx of Giza</b>, and Menkaure's, the smallest of the three great pyramids.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
